From a69c076b5b7c74cf03bad23018f0cb29ec534cb1 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: MeatisOmalley <58967626+MeatisOmalley@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Thu, 15 Oct 2026 06:03:02 -0400
Subject: [PATCH 2/2] N64: Randnet keyboard controller-port device

Adds "Randnet Keyboard" (RND-001) to the N64 controller ports, after the
Console Protocols bus captures (fraser125) cited in ares issue #1482:
- info (0x00/0xFF) answers identifier 0x0002, third byte 0x00;
- 0x13 takes one LED byte (bit 0 Num Lock, 1 Caps Lock, 2 Power) and
  returns three 16-bit key matrix positions plus a status byte (bit 0
  Home, bit 4 more than three keys held, with all slots cleared);
- no reply to 0x01 or other commands.
Key slot order (press order, gaps closed on release, as the MiSTer core
does) and whether Home counts toward the three-key limit are not
verified against hardware. LED changes are shown as status messages.

desktop-ui maps host keys onto the keyboard by position while keyboard
capture is on: a JIS keyboard maps one to one; on a US keyboard the
JIS-only keys use spare keys (Ro: RightControl, Yen: Delete, Muhenkan:
Insert, Henkan: RightAlt, Katakana: RightSuper; Num Lock and the three
keys below the LEDs: PrintScreen, ScrollLock, PageUp, PageDown).
The Windows raw-input keyboard gains Katakana, Ro, Henkan, Muhenkan and
Yen, appended so existing bindings keep their indices.

New settings Nintendo64/ControllerPort1..4 (default Gamepad) pick the
device connected at load: Gamepad, Mouse or Randnet Keyboard. A keyboard
connected this way turns keyboard capture on.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
